Let me look at the `fill` function. `fill.data()` returns `data_` which is a `Char data_[4]` array. `fill.size()` returns `size_`. If `size_` is larger than 4, then `std::copy_n(fill.data(), fill_size, it)` would read out of bounds. But `fill_t::operator=` checks `size > max_size` (4) and throws. So `size_` should be at most 4.

Wait, but there's a subtle issue. Let me look at `fill_t::operator=` again:

```cpp
FMT_CONSTEXPR void operator=(basic_string_view<Char> s) {
    auto size = s.size();
    if (size > max_size) {
      FMT_THROW(format_error("invalid fill"));
      return;
    }
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) data_[i] = s[i];
    size_ = static_cast<unsigned char>(size);
  }
```

This is safe. But wait - the `on_fill` in `specs_setter` does `specs_.fill = fill`. But `specs_.fill` is a `fill_t<Char>`, and `fill` is a `basic_string_view<Char>`. So it calls `fill_t::operator=(basic_string_view)`. OK.

